{
  "result": "The library at the edge of town had a clock that always ran seven minutes slow. Nobody knew whether this was an accident or a decision made long ago, but the librarian maintained that a little extra time was useful for people who loved books more than punctuality ever deserved.\n\nOne rainy afternoon, a boy named Eli arrived just before closing with a paper boat in his pocket. He had sailed it through every puddle on the way, and it was beginning to look less like a boat than a remarkably determined piece of wet laundry. The librarian smiled knowingly.\n\nShe led him to a shelf of books about oceans, where whales crossed blue pages and explorers followed stars. Eli chose a story about an island that appeared only to sailors who had lost their maps. He sat down to read while rain tapped softly against the tall windows outside.\n\nWhen the clock finally announced closing time, Eli looked up, surprised. The librarian stamped his book and placed his soggy boat between two sheets of newspaper. By tomorrow, she said, it might be ready for another voyage. Outside, the rain had stopped, leaving the pavement bright with reflected evening light.\n\nEli walked home carrying the book under his coat. He imagined the hidden island waiting somewhere beyond the ordinary streets. Perhaps getting lost was not always a problem. Perhaps it was sometimes an invitation to notice a door, a puddle, or a clock offering seven unexpected minutes of quiet possibility.",
